---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const rows = [
  ["Spain", "888", "1:52,649", "4,882"],
  ["Uruguay", "431", "1:7,962", "896"],
  ["Argentina", "293", "1:145,882", "12,355"],
  ["Mexico", "223", "1:556,620", "9,555"],
  ["United States", "107", "1:3,387,467", "192,132"],
  ["South Africa", "61", "1:888,159", "57,770"],
  ["Haiti", "49", "1:218,039", "8,036"],
  ["Chile", "14", "1:1,258,320", "22,898"],
  ["France", "11", "1:6,038,429", "280,838"],
];
---
<Base title="The name">
  <h1>The name</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    <em>Lerena</em> is a rare surname — rare enough that rarity itself becomes a research tool. It may also
    be two separate names sharing one spelling. Both facts shape everything else in this archive.
  </p>

  <h2>How rare</h2>
  <p>
    Forebears, accessed 8 September 2026: <strong>2,124 bearers worldwide</strong>, the
    <strong>197,177th</strong> commonest surname on earth, an incidence of <strong>1 in 3,431,048</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Set that beside the other families in this group of archives. <em>Falco</em> has
    <strong>46,749</strong> bearers and two independent origins, so no two Falco lines can be assumed
    related. <em>Lerena</em> is <strong>twenty-two times scarcer</strong>. The methodological consequence
    runs the other way:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    With a surname this rare, a cluster of Lerenas in one town is far more likely to be one family than to
    be coincidence. Connections may be <em>proposed</em> on distribution alone — but they must still be
    <em>proved</em> in the registers.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Where they are</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Bearers</th><th>Incidence</th><th>Rank in country</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(([c, b, i, r]) => (
          <tr><td>{c}</td><td>{b}</td><td class="mono">{i}</td><td class="mono">{r}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small">
    Smaller presences are also recorded in Cuba, Brazil, Peru, Ecuador, Venezuela, England, Ireland,
    Canada, Switzerland, Belgium and the Philippines. Source: Forebears.
  </p>

  <h3>Read the table by density, not by total</h3>
  <p>
    Spain has the largest absolute number, and that is what one would expect of the country of origin.
    But <strong>Uruguay's density is the highest in the world</strong> — 1 in 7,962, against Spain's
    1 in 52,649. A small population carrying a name at six times the homeland's density is the classic
    signature of <strong>founder emigration</strong>: a few people arrived, and their descendants are now a
    measurable fraction of the country.
  </p>
  <p>
    Uruguay and Argentina together make the <strong>River Plate</strong> the true modern heartland of the
    name. The family's own account — Rosario, on the Paraná — sits squarely inside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>South Africa's 61</strong> deserve the same reading. For a surname of 2,124 people worldwide,
    61 in one country far from Iberia and the Americas is not background scatter. It looks like one
    family that arrived and multiplied. <a href={u("/south-africa")}>South Africa &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What the name means — the unresolved fork</h2>
  <p>There are two candidate origins, and the archive does not yet know which applies here.</p>

  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1. Basque: a house-name</h3>
      <p>
        The suffix <strong>-ena</strong> is the Basque possessive, "the house of" or "belonging to" — the
        formative behind <em>Perurena</em>, <em>Marinelarena</em>, <em>Arruabarrena</em> and hundreds more.
        On this reading <em>Lerena</em> is a <strong>house-name</strong>, and in Basque practice the house
        names the family, not the reverse.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        A Basque origin would also fit the destination. Basque emigration to the River Plate began in the
        1820s and ran for a century; it is estimated that up to a tenth of Uruguay's population has at
        least one Basque surname. <span class="chip dis">Plausible, unproven</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">2. Castilian: a town in Extremadura</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Llerena</strong>, in Badajoz, is a real and significant town — seat of the Order of
        Santiago and of an Inquisitorial tribunal, with a large medieval Jewish and <em>converso</em>
        population. It has given its name to <strong>36,828</strong> bearers of <em>Llerena</em>.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Forebears lists <em>Llerena</em> as the closest variant of <em>Lerena</em> at 92% similarity. The
        Castilian <em>Ll-</em> reduces to <em>L-</em> easily in Spanish-American registers and at ports of
        arrival, so a <em>Lerena</em> may simply be a <em>Llerena</em> written down by someone in a hurry.
        <span class="chip dis">Plausible, unproven</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Why the fork matters, and how it gets closed</h3>
  <p>
    It matters because the two answers send the research to opposite ends of Spain — the Basque provinces
    and Navarre, or Badajoz in Extremadura — and to different record systems. It is the same shape of
    problem the Falco archive met in <em>Falco</em> versus <em>De Falco</em>, where clerks moved between
    forms freely and one line could appear under both.
  </p>
  <p>It is closed the same way, and only this way:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Find the family's <strong>earliest River Plate records</strong> and see which spelling they use, and
      whether the two forms alternate for the same person.</li>
    <li>Read the <strong>Spanish provincial distribution</strong> properly. Forebears places the Spanish
      Lerenas chiefly in <strong>La Rioja, Andalusia and Madrid</strong> — which is awkward for both
      theories, being neither Basque nor Extremaduran, though La Rioja borders the Basque country and
      Andalusia is where Extremadurans went. This needs the INE provincial figures, not a summary.</li>
    <li>Find the <strong>emigration record</strong>. A ship's list or a consular registration usually names
      the town of origin outright, and settles it in one line.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small">
    Note also <em>Lerena Lenguas</em>, the double surname of the Argentine Stud Book's co-founder. Both
    halves are River Plate names, and <em>Lenguas</em> is Uruguayan. <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Sources for this page</h2>
  <p class="small">
    Forebears (worldwide and per-country figures, accessed 8 September 2026) · Wikipedia, <em>Basque
    surnames</em> and <em>Llerena, Badajoz</em> · NameCensus. The Basque and Castilian readings are
    standard onomastics, not a claim about this family.
  </p>
</Base>
